-\paragraph{SE reduction ratios.}
-The squeeze-and-excitation bottleneck ratio scales adaptively: Block~1
-(120~ch, $r=8$), Block~2 (180~ch, $r=12$), Blocks~3--4 (146--360~ch,
-$r=12$--16), maintaining SE overhead at 1--2\% of block parameters.
-
-\paragraph{Dropout schedule.}
-Gradual dropout [0.025, 0.025, 0.0375, 0.05] across blocks matches
-receptive field growth: early blocks (local context) tolerate less dropout;
-final blocks (global context) require stronger regularisation.
+\paragraph{Hard-sigmoid SE.}
+Each SE block uses hard-sigmoid excitation, $\sigma_h(x) =
+\text{relu6}(x+3)/6$, which decomposes to Add, ReLU6, and Mul --- all
+basic TFLite Micro ops.  This avoids the exponential required by standard
+sigmoid and improves INT8 quantisation fidelity relative to float sigmoid.
+
+\paragraph{Why no attention.}
+Model~1g, which adds multi-head self-attention (MHSA) to the DS-CNN
+backbone, achieves only $+$0.09~pp over 1e (94.21\% vs.\ 94.12\% INT8).
+More critically, MHSA uses the \texttt{BATCH\_MATMUL} op, which is absent
+from the TFLite Micro op set on Portenta H7.  The marginal accuracy gain
+does not justify MCU incompatibility, so attention is excluded from MynaNet.
+
+\paragraph{Why 5$\times$5 depthwise in deeper blocks.}
+Larger kernels in Blocks~3--4 capture broader spectro-temporal patterns.
+At this stage the spatial dimensions are already small (after three
+MaxPool2D), so the receptive field increase is efficient with minimal
+parameter overhead, as depthwise kernels are shared per channel.
 
